1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,780
There are people living in almost every corner of our crowded planet.
在我们这个拥挤的星球上，几乎每个角落都有人居住。

2
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:16,240
From the tops of mountains to deep underground, we’ve always been able to find habitable places to make a home.
从高山之巅到地下深处，人类总能找到适宜居住的地方安家落户。

3
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:23,970
But with space at a premium, we’ve started to look even deeper for somewhere to live.
但随着空间愈发稀缺，我们开始将目光投向更深的地方，寻找新的栖身之所。

4
00:00:23,970 --> 00:00:30,960
Throughout history, people have been fascinated by the idea of living underwater.
自古以来，人类就对水下居住的构想十分着迷。

5
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:41,070
More recently, the effects of human activity on the land have led some to look to the oceans for natural resources.
近年来，人类活动对陆地造成的影响，让一些人开始将目光投向海洋，挖掘其中的自然资源。

6
00:00:41,070 --> 00:00:54,520
Oceanographers and architects have looked at designing vessels, labs and underwater habitats to enable people to live and work in a submerged world.
海洋学家和建筑师们着手设计潜水器、实验室以及水下栖息地，希望能让人们在水下世界生活和工作。

7
00:00:54,520 --> 00:01:04,950
But, while dreams of an aquatic city have remained a fantasy, how close are we to making our home on the seabed a reality?
不过，尽管水上城市的梦想至今仍停留在幻想阶段，但我们距离在海床安家落户的现实还有多远呢？

8
00:01:04,950 --> 00:01:13,640
The pioneer of undersea living was Ian Koblick, sometimes described as an ‘aquanaut’.
水下居住的先驱者是伊恩·科布利克，他有时也被称为“海底居民”。

9
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:23,420
He claimed technology already existed to create underwater colonies supporting up to a hundred people.
他声称，现有的技术已经能够建造可容纳上百人的水下聚居地。

10
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:34,410
He even opened La Chalupa, once the largest and most advanced underwater habitat and research facility in the world.
他还曾创办了“拉查鲁帕”水下基地，这里一度是全球规模最大、技术最先进的水下栖息地与研究设施。

11
00:01:34,410 --> 00:01:40,980
A more recent and ambitious design for an underwater city is Ocean Spiral.
“海洋螺旋”是近年来一个更为宏大的水下城市设计方案。

12
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:49,590
Designed by the Japanese Shimizu Corporation, it’s a huge transparent globe attached to the seabed.
这个方案由日本清水建设株式会社设计，主体是一个固定在海床上的巨大透明球体。

13
00:01:49,590 --> 00:02:00,610
It’s got a tower running through it to the surface, providing space for homes, offices and even an amusement park for five thousand residents.
球体内有一座贯穿上下、直达海面的塔楼，可为五千名居民提供住宅、办公区域，甚至还有一座游乐园。

14
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:17,250
Masaki Takeuchi from the company told the BBC: “Our idea is to connect the sea surface and the deep sea vertically, and that way we believe that we can utilise the capability of the deep sea.”
该公司的竹内正树在接受BBC采访时表示：“我们的构想是将海面与深海垂直连接起来，我们相信通过这种方式，能够充分开发深海的潜在价值。”

15
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:23,200
Of course, there are engineering challenges in constructing an oceanic metropolis.
当然，建造一座海洋大都市面临着诸多工程难题。

16
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:34,360
And living underwater would have major effects on the human body, especially as there is an increase in atmospheric pressure which could lead to the bends.
而且水下生活也会对人体产生重大影响，尤其是水下气压的升高可能会导致潜水员患上减压病。

17
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:43,180
So, maybe it’s too soon to be packing our bags and setting up a new life three thousand metres under the sea!
如此看来，现在就打包行李，去三千米深的海底开启新生活，或许还为时过早。
